# Secret injection for this repo — deliberately NOT `op inject`.
#
# The only thing that lives in the ambient shell environment is a per-project
# 1Password *service account* token. Secret VALUES are never exported here.
# Everything that needs a real secret resolves it lazily, per command, via
# `op run --env-file=.env.tpl -- ...` inside the Makefile: op reads the
# VAR=op://... lines from .env.tpl, resolves them against this token, and
# injects the values into that one child process only.
#
# Why this shape:
#   - Nothing long-lived: secrets exist for the lifetime of a single command,
#     not for the lifetime of the shell (or of an agent session that inherited
#     it). `printenv` in this directory shows the token and nothing else.
#   - `op run` masks the child's stdout/stderr, so a stray `echo` of a
#     plaintext value does not leak it. Masking is a literal plaintext match
#     only — it does not survive base64 or any other encoding — so it is a
#     backstop, never the sole protection. See the Makefile's note above
#     diff-homelab.
#   - The service account is scoped to just the vaults this project needs.
#
# So: the absence of `set -a` + `op inject` below is intentional, not an
# unfinished edit. Do not "restore" it. If `make apply-homelab` complains that a
# var is MISSING, the fix is a line in .env.tpl, not an export here.
#
# Pattern: https://blog.cynexia.com/updated-using-1password-and-direnv-to-store-developer-secrets/

# Fail loudly and immediately if the token does not load. Without this check a
# failed `op read` exports an empty string, direnv reports success, and the
# problem surfaces much later as 25 confusing `MISSING:` lines from
# `make require-vars` — pointing at .env.tpl instead of at the real cause.
# Note the assignment is separate from the `export`: `export VAR="$(cmd)"`
# returns export's status, not the command's, so a failing `op read` would go
# unnoticed. Neither branch prints the token or its length.
if ! _op_token="$(op read 'op://homelab/1pw/service_account_key')" || [ -z "$_op_token" ]; then
  _msg="1Password service account token did not load (op://homelab/1pw/service_account_key).
  Check: 'op whoami' for an authenticated session, network reachability, and
  that the item/field still exists. No secrets are available until this works."
  if command -v log_error >/dev/null 2>&1; then log_error "$_msg"; else echo "ERROR: $_msg" >&2; fi
  unset _op_token _msg
  exit 1
fi

export OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN="$_op_token"
unset _op_token
